\subsubsection{Sensitivity and degradation cases}
\label{sec:ou-robustness}
The robustness study is retained as a sensitivity analysis of the deployed
OU--III architecture rather than as a comparison among alternative adaptation
laws. At the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ operating point, one-factor-at-a-time
(OFAT) sweeps perturb $\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, or $r_S$ by multipliers $0.5$,
$0.75$, $1.0$, $1.25$, and $1.5$ while holding the other two quantities fixed.
These three sweeps isolate the direct process-covariance and integral-state
regularization sensitivities and are independent of how an online adaptation
law couples the parameters.

Two additional coupled sweeps evaluate the same perturbations along the
\emph{deployed SpectralMSE manifold}. With the physical acceleration scale
perturbed by a factor $c$ at fixed $\tau$ and cadence,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{r_S(c\sigma_a,\tau)}{r_S(\sigma_a,\tau)}=c^{6/7}.
 \label{eq:robustness-sigma-coupling}
\end{equation}
For a correlation-time perturbation $\tau\mapsto c\tau$ at fixed acceleration
scale,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{r_S(c\tau)}{r_S(\tau)}
 =c^{24/7}\sqrt{\frac{T_S(\tau)}{T_S(c\tau)}}.
 \label{eq:robustness-tau-coupling}
\end{equation}
Away from the pseudo-update cadence clamps, $T_S\propto\tau$ and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:robustness-tau-coupling} reduces to
$r_S\mapsto c^{41/14}r_S$. Thus the coupled sensitivity experiment is retained,
but it now follows the analytical law actually used by the filter.

The committed robustness archive predates SpectralMSE. Its three OFAT columns
remain valid because the other two tuning quantities were fixed in each sweep;
its old coupled columns are not reused as current evidence. Once the robustness
bundle is regenerated by the current driver, the publication automatically
replaces the OFAT-only table by the five-column SpectralMSE study and restores
its sensitivity figure.

